WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:01.560
[ANNOUNCEMENT]

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:14.920
JEFFREY TOOBIN: Hello, everyone. Should we
get started? Wait, there's someone coming

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.139
in.

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:22.890
Well, welcome. I am Jeffrey Toobin. I'm happy
to be the moderator today.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.280
Today's meeting of the Council on Foreign
Relations is called "The Synthesis of Law

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.860
and Politics in the -- and the Evolution of
International Justice."

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:37.480
Please completely turn off, not -- vibrate
is not good enough. Please turn off your electronic

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:45.410
devices. This meeting is on the record, and
the next meeting of the council is for CFR

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:50.930
fellow Richard Betts and his new book, "American
Force: Dangers, Delusions, and Dilemmas in

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:54.910
National Security," on February 13th.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:01:02.360
Our occasion today is mostly to celebrate
David's new book. David Scheffer is the Mayer

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.710
Brown/Robert A. Helman Professor of Law and
director of the Center for International Human

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:12.240
Rights at Northwestern Law School. He is the
former U.S. ambassador-at-large for war crimes

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.140
issues in the State Department in the second
term of President Clinton and is the author

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.649
of -- show the book; it's always important
to show the book -- "All the Missing Souls"

00:01:21.649 --> 00:01:22.649
--

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:23.649
&gt;&gt;MR. : Buy the book.

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:27.710
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: -- buy the book; even better -- "A
Personal History of the War Crimes Tribunal."

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:34.290
David was named one of the top global thinkers
of 2011 by Foreign Policy magazine. He was

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:42.511
recently named as the adviser to the secretary-general
on war crimes issues in Cambodia, because

00:01:42.511 --> 00:01:47.270
his former jobs were not depressing enough.
(Laughter.)

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:53.170
John Bellinger is an adjunct senior fellow
at international and national security law

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.720
at the Council on Foreign Relations. He's
a partner at Arnold &amp; Porter. And he was the

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.170
legal adviser in the State Department in the
Bush administration.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:09.979
So, David, let's -- the book is basically
about your tenure as the ambassador-at-large

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:15.590
for war crimes issues. You were the first
person in that job. That position did not

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:16.590
exist before.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:25.410
So today, looking back and looking forward,
is a war criminal at risk of prosecution today?

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:31.200
&gt;&gt;DAVID SCHEFFER: Oh, I think far more today
than in the past. I think everything's relative.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.379
There will still be an enormous range of political
and military leaders who will escape the jaws

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:41.930
of justice, so there's no question about that.
But I think if you look at what has happened

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:48.980
in the last 17 years, beginning in 1993 and
reaching to the present day, I think we do

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:54.700
see the beginning of the end of official impunity.
We're certainly not there yet. But I think

00:02:54.700 --> 00:03:00.800
the idea that political and military leaders
can actually plot genocide, crimes against

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:10.190
humanity or massive war crimes and assume
that there is some pass or exclusion from

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:16.310
any liability simply because of their official
status -- those days, I think, are starting

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:22.590
to come to an end. And we have a track record
now of a number of leaders who in fact have

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:27.090
faced justice since 1993, before these war
crimes tribunals.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:32.150
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: And we've had war crimes tribunals.
The International Court of -- the International

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:37.950
Criminal Court exists. What do you think has
been the most important thing in creating

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:46.060
a world where there is at least the possibility
of legal action against perpetrators of war

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.060
crimes?

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:54.200
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Well, I think the most important
thing is that hopefully it will begin to influence

00:03:54.200 --> 00:04:00.080
the character of certain policy-making. In
other words, if we can, over the generations

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.550
and over the decades, understand that these
courts -- and particularly the permanent International

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:11.180
Criminal Court does exist -- and that if one
steps over a particular line in terms of how

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:19.070
you conduct either state policy or rebel policy,
that you will subject yourself at least to

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:25.529
the scrutiny, if not the official investigation,
of a criminal court, if you in fact veer into

00:04:25.529 --> 00:04:30.289
criminal conduct, I think that's a significant
step.

00:04:30.289 --> 00:04:35.969
After World War II, we had a momentary experience
at Nuremberg and Tokyo with this, and it was

00:04:35.969 --> 00:04:42.699
very dynamic and very pointed and very significant
at that time. But it faded, I think, from

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:48.339
our memory throughout the entire Cold War,
and I think it's a constructive step that

00:04:48.339 --> 00:04:53.580
over the last 17 years we've started to resurrect
that but in a -- in a quite different way

00:04:53.580 --> 00:05:00.490
and, I think, a more meaningful way internationally.
It's not precisely victor's justice. I think

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:06.569
oftentimes it probably can be viewed as the
justice of the powerful over the weak. I think

00:05:06.569 --> 00:05:12.849
that's still of some significance in the system.
And the permanent International Criminal Court

00:05:12.849 --> 00:05:18.169
is designed to, I think, try to even get away
from that formula to a more balanced approach

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:19.490
to justice.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:25.779
But there are certain, you know, geopolitical
realities that obviously make that a very

00:05:25.779 --> 00:05:28.629
qualified, you know, prediction.

00:05:28.629 --> 00:05:33.460
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: What has -- but I meant sort of
-- I mean, institutionally, what's worked

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.000
best? The regional tribunals --

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.000
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Oh. Oh, I see --

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.240
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: -- or the criminal court or what
institutions have been most successful?

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:48.689
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Well, I suppose so far the verdict
on the International Criminal Court is out.

00:05:48.689 --> 00:05:53.419
We don't even have a single conviction yet
or a judgment for the ICC.

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:59.159
But if you look at the work product of the
International Criminal Tribunal for the Former

00:05:59.159 --> 00:06:04.559
Yugoslavia, for Rwanda, the special court
for Sierra Leone -- those three courts, I

00:06:04.559 --> 00:06:09.339
suppose you could say, are regional courts.
They're -- all three of them are international

00:06:09.339 --> 00:06:16.270
courts. And although each one of them had
particular difficulties and problems both

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:21.909
in their creation, in the early years of their
operation, I think at the end of the day you

00:06:21.909 --> 00:06:28.409
have to conclude that they achieved their
primary objectives of bringing to justice

00:06:28.409 --> 00:06:33.780
the leaders of atrocities in those regions
during particular periods of time. That limited

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.470
objective can be extremely useful and efficient
for the international system. The problem

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:45.679
of course is the cost and the political capital
that has to be expended every time you approach

00:06:45.679 --> 00:06:50.969
one of these issues from a strictly regional
basis with a newly created regional court.

00:06:50.969 --> 00:06:56.680
It's a very, very difficult process, which
is one of the reasons of course the ICC, the

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.330
International Criminal Court, was negotiated
in the 1990s, was to try to provide actually

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:06.569
some efficiency in the system. And we have
to still see whether that will bear true.

00:07:06.569 --> 00:07:13.259
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: One of the most interesting chapters
in your book is what you call American exceptionalism,

00:07:13.259 --> 00:07:19.830
and much of what your book is about is the
intersection of American foreign policy with

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:25.749
whole issue of war crimes. I mean, without
telling the story of your whole book, what

00:07:25.749 --> 00:07:32.729
is -- I think you have said that it had -- that
being American is both the best thing and

00:07:32.729 --> 00:07:37.800
the worst thing about being involved in this
field. Describe what you mean by that.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:43.279
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Being the lead American negotiator
on these war crimes tribunals was a tremendously,

00:07:43.279 --> 00:07:53.800
shall we say, influential and occasionally
quite powerful position to be in. It is -- and

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:58.119
many of you in this room have been negotiators
for the U.S. government, so you know exactly

00:07:58.119 --> 00:08:02.839
what I'm talking about. You do bring the weight
of your most powerful nation in the world

00:08:02.839 --> 00:08:11.990
into the room. And that is extremely significant.
You can change things. You can work compromises.

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:17.279
You can bring that influence to bear like
no other nation.

00:08:17.279 --> 00:08:22.439
But at the same time, I was bringing in that
other sort of exceptional characteristic of

00:08:22.439 --> 00:08:28.830
the United States, which is that we're a globally
deployed military power. Our military dwarfs

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:33.469
all other militaries. And so for -- it's particularly
for an issue such as this, where you're talking

00:08:33.469 --> 00:08:38.490
about war crimes, crimes against humanity
and genocide, which so often involve the use

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:47.790
of military force and commanded military forces
in the field -- you can -- you can quickly

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:53.959
find yourself in a situation, as the United
States did in the 1990s, in these negotiations,

00:08:53.959 --> 00:09:00.449
where exposing ourselves to this kind of liability,
particularly since it's highly sensitized

00:09:00.449 --> 00:09:07.940
with our global military presence, becomes
a very, very difficult equation to work out

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:09.709
in the negotiating room.

00:09:09.709 --> 00:09:15.580
I think I have one line in there where I say,
in terms of the ICC negotiations, instead

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:23.750
of being the slayer of impunity, I became
its champion at times, because I was seeking

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:30.810
a certain level of impunity for U.S. military
forces. And that became a very difficult equation

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:38.160
to negotiate, paradoxical and almost hypocritical
at times, and it can undermine and sap your

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:39.779
strength in the negotiating room.

00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:41.670
Ultimately, it did. Ultimately, it did.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:47.290
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Well, before we turn to John, if
we could just move the story forward a little

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:56.649
bit, which is, describe what you saw in the
Bush administration. What became of your work?

00:09:56.649 --> 00:10:03.670
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Well, on the one hand, on the
International Criminal Court ledger, I thought

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:09.889
it was a -- it was a very regressive period,
the first term of the Bush administration.

00:10:09.889 --> 00:10:18.980
There was a sort of a de-signing of my signature
of the Rome Statute, to -- so to speak. And

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:19.980
-- yeah.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.899
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Well, could you -- for those of
us who are not particularly well-versed in

00:10:22.899 --> 00:10:29.120
that issue, describe -- just as a factual
matter, what is the American position with

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:30.899
regard to the International Criminal Court?

00:10:30.899 --> 00:10:33.170
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Ah, well, it depends on what year
you're asking. (Laughter.)

00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:34.170
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Well --

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:35.829
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: But let me also say -- I can answer
the question, though.

00:10:35.829 --> 00:10:36.829
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: -- since the '90s --

00:10:36.829 --> 00:10:42.940
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: I -- yeah, I can answer the question.
President Clinton publicly stated six times,

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:48.399
on the public record, that he wanted to see
a criminal -- an international criminal court

00:10:48.399 --> 00:10:54.019
built, and he wanted it built by the end of
the 20th century. So guess what my instructions

00:10:54.019 --> 00:10:56.320
were: Build the court.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:02.721
So I was there to negotiate the actual building
of an international criminal court and to

00:11:02.721 --> 00:11:08.140
have the United States as the supporting pillar
of that court. The question at the end of

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:13.399
the day and at Rome in 1998 was precisely
what kind of court with what kind of jurisdiction;

00:11:13.399 --> 00:11:18.959
how would it be triggered, et cetera. All
of that became rather complicated.

00:11:18.959 --> 00:11:25.449
But the hope for such a court remained within
the Clinton administration, and by the end

00:11:25.449 --> 00:11:32.610
of December of 2000 I was in a position, at
the president's instruction, to actually sign

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:36.820
the Rome Statute and at least indicate that
we're there as a signatory. We said that we

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:40.779
still have a lot of work to do on this treaty,
because there were -- there were years of

00:11:40.779 --> 00:11:49.730
supplemental negotiations still to take place.
And even President Clinton today is -- when

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:54.810
you look at what he has said since he was
in office to the -- to today, you know that

00:11:54.810 --> 00:12:01.500
he still has great support for the creation
and the working of the International Criminal

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:02.500
Court.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:08.300
As far as the United States is concerned,
however -- and I know John will, I think,

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:12.889
support me in this -- is that there's a -- not
only just a fractured view of whether the

00:12:12.889 --> 00:12:18.759
United States should be in the International
Criminal Court, but a view of whether we should

00:12:18.759 --> 00:12:23.870
be engaged at all with the work of the court.
And I think this is where I think there's

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:31.399
been great progress. Ever since 2005 when
we abstained on the referral of Darfur to

00:12:31.399 --> 00:12:37.009
the ICC and in the second term of the Bush
administration, there were increasing steps

00:12:37.009 --> 00:12:41.149
of informal cooperation and of communication
with the court.

00:12:41.149 --> 00:12:46.980
And in recent years there's been a significant
amount of U.S. coordination and cooperation

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:51.710
with the court, even to the extent last October
of a hundred U.S. military servicemen being

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:57.839
deployed to Uganda to work with the Ugandan
military for the express purpose of tracking

00:12:57.839 --> 00:13:02.680
and apprehending the indicted fugitives of
the Lord's Resistance Army, who need to stand

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:08.389
trial in The Hague. And I -- and that is -- that
is the United States using some of its own

00:13:08.389 --> 00:13:12.700
military assets to achieve a very key objective
of the ICC.

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:18.759
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: So let me just move to John, because
I don't want to neglect you completely. What

00:13:18.759 --> 00:13:25.819
did you feel the Bush administration's attitude
towards war crimes tribunals was? What -- how

00:13:25.819 --> 00:13:31.379
did you define yourselves sort of objectively
in the world and versus the Clinton administration?

00:13:31.379 --> 00:13:36.860
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: Yeah, well -- and let me take
that sort of in two pieces, because the -- there

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:41.510
are the ad hoc tribunals, which David mostly
talks about in his book before he gets to

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:46.079
the ICC, and then there's the International
Criminal Court, which is a different kettle

00:13:46.079 --> 00:13:47.709
of fish. And let me say a word about each
of those.

00:13:47.709 --> 00:13:51.569
I mean, first, I think almost everybody in
the room knows, if you know David -- I mean,

00:13:51.569 --> 00:13:57.740
he really deserves tremendous credit for the
work on -- particularly on the ad hoc tribunals,

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:04.230
but also trying to pull together the whole
Rome Statute. And with respect to the ad hoc

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:07.879
tribunals, I think the Bush administration
really just continued that policy.

00:14:07.879 --> 00:14:12.249
If there's one thing I really disagreed in
the book -- although I'd confess I've not

00:14:12.249 --> 00:14:17.899
read all of it, but I did read page 417 -- (chuckles)
-- in which he said that he felt that the

00:14:17.899 --> 00:14:23.209
Bush administration -- it has essentially
ignored the ad hoc tribunals. I actually would

00:14:23.209 --> 00:14:27.660
invite David maybe to take back those words,
because I think it's really not true. We actually

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:32.720
built on what David had done, largely because
his former deputy, Pierre Prosper, then became

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:35.240
our war crimes prosecutor.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:43.459
But we were tremendously supportive of the
three ad hoc tribunals, the ICTY for Yugoslavia,

00:14:43.459 --> 00:14:49.230
Rwanda and the Special Court for Sierra Leone.
We poured millions of dollars, $300 million

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:56.550
into the ad hoc tribunals. We provided tremendous
diplomatic and operational and intelligence

00:14:56.550 --> 00:15:01.959
support. I candidly think -- but I can't really
compare directly -- but Secretary Powell and

00:15:01.959 --> 00:15:07.139
Secretary Rice were more supportive of these
tribunals and Pierre Prosper's and our efforts

00:15:07.139 --> 00:15:13.220
maybe even than some of the work David had
behind him. I mean, they were out hauling

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:20.850
up world leaders, insisting that there be
cooperation with the -- with the ICTY, with

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:25.089
the ICTR, the Special Court for Sierra Leone.

00:15:25.089 --> 00:15:29.459
Just to give you one example to make it concrete
of how these things actually work, to have

00:15:29.459 --> 00:15:37.660
high-level political support, we knew Charles
Taylor was in -- was being essentially protected

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:45.360
in Nigeria. We were putting pressure on Obasanjo,
the president of Nigeria, to push him out

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:50.769
so that he could be brought before the Special
Court for Sierra Leone. Obasanjo desperately

00:15:50.769 --> 00:15:55.990
wanted to have a meeting in the Oval Office,
which we basically agreed to because Nigeria

00:15:55.990 --> 00:16:01.720
was a(n) ally. And then in the midst of all
of this, Charles Taylor sort of disappeared

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:07.139
from our clutches. And Secretary Rice then
made it very clear to Obasanjo that there

00:16:07.139 --> 00:16:13.170
would be no Oval Office visit for him until
Charles Taylor reappeared. And magically,

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:18.390
Charles Taylor reappeared -- (laughter) -- and
was picked up and delivered to the Special

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:20.230
Court for Sierra Leone.

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:26.610
So the main point overall is that with respect
to the ad hoc tribunals, I think in the Bush

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:31.059
administration, I would say the policy was
essentially the same. I spent lots and lots

00:16:31.059 --> 00:16:37.160
of hours in the Situation Room at the White
House working on ways to bring Milosevic and

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:45.040
Karadzic and Gotovina and Charles Taylor to
justice before these international tribunals.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:49.470
Philosophically -- and I'll stop and sort
of go back here, and we'll get back to the

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:54.260
ICC in a minute -- you know, I do think that
there was probably more of a feeling in the

00:16:54.260 --> 00:17:02.100
Bush administration that international justice
ought to be done locally and regionally where

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:11.970
possible, more reliance on nation-states than
the default norm being an international institution.

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:18.850
But with respect to these ad hoc tribunals,
where there really was no other way of -- the

00:17:18.850 --> 00:17:23.640
administration, from Secretary Powell and
Rice to the president -- hugely supportive

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:25.990
of those three ad hocs.

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:28.770
I'll stop, and we can come back to the ICC
-- (inaudible) -- talk about that.

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:29.770
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Well, why don't you respond to --

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:33.919
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: To respond to -- I think we have
a misunderstanding, because I didn't actually

00:17:33.919 --> 00:17:39.240
write that on page 417. (Laughter.) But that's
OK. I -- John, it's just fine.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:45.070
I actually support so much of what John just
said. The only thing that I would -- on these

00:17:45.070 --> 00:17:49.179
ad hocs that I would take a little bit of
exception with was I think there was a period

00:17:49.179 --> 00:17:56.149
in 2001 when we took our eye off the ball
on trying to get the Special Court for Sierra

00:17:56.149 --> 00:18:02.051
Leone moving, as well as the Extraordinary
Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia. But it's

00:18:02.051 --> 00:18:08.760
just a tactic of -- I mean, it's just an issue
of tactics during that first year of the administration

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:14.270
when, obviously, there was so much on -- you
know, coming at you that was -- that had to

00:18:14.270 --> 00:18:17.020
be dealt with.

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:25.710
The point I make on page 417 is that this
book concerns events that preceded the so-called

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:30.919
war on terror, but the exceptional phenomenon
of the Bush administration years is how little

00:18:30.919 --> 00:18:35.750
impact either the court-building leadership
of the United States during the 1990s or the

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:41.250
growing jurisprudence of those tribunals had
on the strategists of the Bush era, meaning

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:48.120
the strategists for the so-called war on terror
and the impact that the jurisprudence of the

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:54.919
tribunal should have had on their understanding
of international law as it relates to issues

00:18:54.919 --> 00:18:57.659
such as torture and interrogation techniques.

00:18:57.659 --> 00:19:00.840
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Well, John, let me ask you about
that, because sort of the undercurrent -- and

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:06.830
David mentioned it a little bit -- is, you
know, how do we construct international institutions

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:13.131
that -- when we are the country that is sending
troops out into the world and, you know, our

00:19:13.131 --> 00:19:19.690
leaders are -- you know, we're -- are we exposing
American soldiers? Are we exposing Secretary

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:28.850
Rumsfeld to criminal liability in a way that
we don't want? Or are we simply -- not avoiding

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:33.380
criminal responsibility for American leaders
who should have it? And it's a good segue

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:37.610
into the International Criminal Court, which
is a fascinating discussion that sort of spanned

00:19:37.610 --> 00:19:38.610
both administrations.

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:42.500
I mean, you know, the -- sort of the sad thing
is that the whole idea of an international

00:19:42.500 --> 00:19:47.451
criminal court was one that went way back
into the -- you know, the first Bush administration

00:19:47.451 --> 00:19:53.260
or even earlier, and Republicans and Democrats
had historically supported the idea of an

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:57.920
international criminal court, and it's certainly
more efficient than having all of these various

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:05.169
ad hoc tribunals. And I think a Republican
Congress back in the 1980s had said we endorse

00:20:05.169 --> 00:20:10.310
the idea of an international criminal court,
provided that it meets American standards

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:12.120
of due process and is fair.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:15.820
And what happened, I think -- you know, David
was there, and so it's really more his story

00:20:15.820 --> 00:20:24.870
-- was that it went off the rails at Rome
and the -- our military got so concerned that

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:30.950
the court would actually be used against them
that they tied David's hands so that, you

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:35.559
know, we had to be one of only seven countries
in the world that actually voted against the

00:20:35.559 --> 00:20:38.170
Rome Statute.

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:43.880
And there has been that concern that even
though, you know, we would like -- and I think

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:47.320
the Republican Party even is sort of split
on this. I mean, there are some who support

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:52.679
the concept of international criminal justice
but are concerned as to how it is applied

00:20:52.679 --> 00:20:57.370
to us. There are certainly others -- I would
put John Bolton, I think, in this camp -- that

00:20:57.370 --> 00:21:02.419
just really don't like the idea of an international
judicial institution to begin with.

00:21:02.419 --> 00:21:06.179
Let me then say what happened with the International
Criminal Court in the Bush administration,

00:21:06.179 --> 00:21:12.179
because I find it kind of fascinating, in
that perceptions, I think, overtook reality.

00:21:12.179 --> 00:21:16.399
There's certainly a perception that almost
everybody in this room knows -- and particularly

00:21:16.399 --> 00:21:21.620
if you're in Europe -- that the Bush administration
sort of completely changed policy and pursued

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:28.429
an entirely new policy. I would say that's
not really true; that there was a tonal change,

00:21:28.429 --> 00:21:35.870
which I concede. But on the last day, as Bush
-- or as David talks about in his book -- in

00:21:35.870 --> 00:21:40.799
which the Rome Statute was open for signature,
President Clinton, having voted against the

00:21:40.799 --> 00:21:48.500
statute, authorized David to sign, so we signed;
but at the same time, he stated that he would

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:53.510
not send the treaty to the Senate, and recommended
that his successor, who at that point was

00:21:53.510 --> 00:21:57.891
known -- President Bush -- not send it to
the Senate -- so in other words, saying: We're

00:21:57.891 --> 00:21:59.669
not going to become a party.

00:21:59.669 --> 00:22:04.390
So he essentially tried to have it both ways,
which we diplomats often try to do to appeal

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:08.670
to different audiences. I would frankly say
that was sort of the diplomatic equivalent

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:13.730
of smoking without inhaling -- (laughter)
-- that, you know, we were going to sign,

00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:16.419
but we were saying we would not send it to
the Senate.

00:22:16.419 --> 00:22:21.250
What President Bush did, I think, candidly
-- although I recognize the huge concern and

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:24.669
problems that it caused in the world -- was
frankly a little bit more honest about it.

00:22:24.669 --> 00:22:30.720
He then sent a two-line letter to the U.N.
that said: We're not going to become party.

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:36.250
That's -- there really was no unsigning; you
can't unsign a treaty. He simply said formally

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:38.470
what President Clinton had already said informally.

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:44.460
Now, you know, I concede -- and this caused
us big problems that I actually had to then

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:49.509
work on -- is that everybody, particularly
in Europe, felt that we were then trying to

00:22:49.509 --> 00:22:55.820
-- out to kill the court, and that this unsigning
was the first step towards trying to throttle

00:22:55.820 --> 00:23:03.140
the baby in the infancy. And coddle -- and
coupled with this very ICC -- very anti-ICC

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:08.940
piece of legislation, the American Service-Members'
Protection Act, it appeared that the Bush

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:10.330
administration was out to kill it.

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:15.090
Now, as David says in his book, what was interesting
about the ASPA, the American Service-Members'

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:22.100
Protection Act, which authorized the president
to invade The Hague to free any American who

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:27.540
was imprisoned there -- this was all blamed
on President Bush, as if he had personally

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:31.500
written all of this. We didn't write this
piece of legislation. We didn't want this

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:37.919
piece of legislation. I actually negotiated
to try to cut out a lot of it. The -- this

00:23:37.919 --> 00:23:45.470
was written by Republicans in the House who
were concerned on behalf of our military about

00:23:45.470 --> 00:23:47.070
the ICC.

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:51.139
And one of the funny little quirks of history,
(which I now know she wishes she didn't do

00:23:51.139 --> 00:23:55.919
it ?) -- David mentions it in the book -- is
Hillary Clinton voted for the American Service-Members'

00:23:55.919 --> 00:24:02.231
Protection Act, this very anti-ICC piece of
legislation that authorized the president

00:24:02.231 --> 00:24:06.100
to invade The Hague. But it was all blamed
on President Bush.

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:09.960
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: David, do you want to talk about
that?

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:13.529
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Well, yes. (Laughter.)

00:24:13.529 --> 00:24:20.070
I just want to state what I think is a clarification
here, and of course, I explain this at -- in

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:26.200
some detail in the book, that the dynamics
and the rationale of what happened on that

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:32.970
signing date of December 31st, 2000, where,
you know, the president only had three weeks

00:24:32.970 --> 00:24:37.840
left in office at that point, so it's utterly
implausible that you would submit a treaty

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:42.380
of that character, which requires an enormous
amount of implementing legislation, to the

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:46.570
Senate within three weeks -- usually, the
State Department Legal Adviser's Office -- Conrad

00:24:46.570 --> 00:24:51.110
(sp), you might want to confirm this, as well
as my colleague John -- that, you know, it

00:24:51.110 --> 00:24:54.780
usually takes a considerable amount of time
before you turn a treaty around and submit

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:55.780
it.

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:59.809
But I don't want to rest my laurels on that
technical point. What the president was saying

00:24:59.809 --> 00:25:03.630
-- and he said this very clearly in the statement
-- was: I'm not going to submit it to the

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:08.059
Senate; there are still issues that have to
be resolved; we have to meet those issues

00:25:08.059 --> 00:25:12.700
in order to make it eligible for submission
for ratification.

00:25:12.700 --> 00:25:16.730
So there were issues to be resolved and, of
course, they weren't going to be resolved

00:25:16.730 --> 00:25:21.820
immediately. But we did feel that, as being
a signatory state -- in other words, it wasn't

00:25:21.820 --> 00:25:26.639
a rejection of ultimately ratifying the treaty.
That's what was -- not what was being said.

00:25:26.639 --> 00:25:31.930
It was a recognition that we still have issues
that need to be resolved; we think we can

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:37.960
do so in supplemental negotiations. And that
was supposed to be my job, if we had won the

00:25:37.960 --> 00:25:42.360
White House for the next term under Al Gore,
but of course we hadn't. But we were --

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:43.360
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: Exactly.

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:48.779
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: We were signaling to -- (laughter)
-- we were signaling to George W. Bush's administration:

00:25:48.779 --> 00:25:54.620
Look, we have set this up to protect American
interests. They'll be best protected if we're

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:59.190
a signatory state with the credibility of
a signatory state to pursue those interests

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:03.320
in the next term. It was a gamble, granted,
and of course, it wasn't really picked up

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:07.070
by the next administration; but nonetheless,
it was there.

00:26:07.070 --> 00:26:12.350
On the American Service-Members' Protection
Act, yes, a fair number of Democratic senators

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:21.139
did vote for it; a fair number did not; likewise
on the House side. But John is absolutely

00:26:21.139 --> 00:26:26.929
right, you know, in 2002, this was a Jesse
Helms/Tom DeLay bill, and it was in 2000.

00:26:26.929 --> 00:26:34.799
We beat it back in 2000; it was resurrected
in 2002 and it prevailed for a vote.

00:26:34.799 --> 00:26:40.330
And the important thing for all of you to
understand about this law is that the punitive

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:46.340
measures of it have all been repealed by Congress
-- a Republican Congress -- because it's understood

00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:50.950
that they're undercutting other interests
of the United States. The only real punitive

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:56.679
measure left is the one John mentioned, which
is the widest -- I'm sure I'll be corrected

00:26:56.679 --> 00:27:01.581
when I say this -- but the widest grant of
war powers I've ever seen to the president

00:27:01.581 --> 00:27:06.980
of the United States: He has prior approval
to invade The Netherlands, without coming

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:08.799
back to Congress. (Laughter.)

00:27:08.799 --> 00:27:11.630
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: They -- I've got to tell a quick
story on that one --

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:12.630
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Yeah.

00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:15.010
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: -- which may be -- which may
be apocryphal, so someone can correct me,

00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:19.529
but I -- the received wisdom in the White
House was that after the American Service-Members'

00:27:19.529 --> 00:27:24.179
Protection Act and the "invade The Hague"
provision, the new Dutch ambassador came in

00:27:24.179 --> 00:27:28.940
to present his credentials. And you know,
they always make small talk in the Oval Office.

00:27:28.940 --> 00:27:32.610
And apparently, the Dutch ambassador was actually
fairly honest; said: You know, &gt;&gt;MR. President,

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:36.179
I've got to tell you candidly, there's great
concern in my country about this new bill

00:27:36.179 --> 00:27:41.860
that you've just signed that authorizes you
to invade The Hague. And the president apparently

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:46.700
wrapped his arm around him and said: I wouldn't
worry about it. (Laughs, laughter.)

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:50.149
The -- let me say a word about the -- (inaudible)
-- term, though, because that does sort of

00:27:50.149 --> 00:27:56.470
get to where we are. You know, candidly, because
of these perceptions that the Bush administration,

00:27:56.470 --> 00:28:02.590
you know, was really trying to kill the court
and therefore didn't actually believe in international

00:28:02.590 --> 00:28:07.399
justice or accountability at all -- despite,
as I've said, all of the support that we were

00:28:07.399 --> 00:28:12.030
providing for the ad hoc tribunals and the
fact that we clearly shared the goal of the

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:16.330
International Criminal Court, we were simply
concerned about how it would be applied to

00:28:16.330 --> 00:28:21.430
Americans, and that it might be applied to
Americans even though we weren't a party -- there

00:28:21.430 --> 00:28:25.570
then became this perception in the second
term, that many people know here, that we

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:26.870
were really out to kill the court.

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:31.539
You know, that caused me -- and I had spent
my first term at the White House at the National

00:28:31.539 --> 00:28:36.929
Security Council -- some angst, that the Bush
administration looked anti-international justice;

00:28:36.929 --> 00:28:41.150
which is really the point of sort of what
we're talking about here. So I, at the end

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:46.220
of 2004, after we won the election, wrote
a transition paper for Secretary Rice; said:

00:28:46.220 --> 00:28:49.919
We really need to correct the record here.
You know, we're not going to change policy

00:28:49.919 --> 00:28:53.930
and become a member of the ICC -- that's even
where the Clinton administration was -- but

00:28:53.930 --> 00:29:00.990
we need to correct the perception that we
can have common ground. And we worked then

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:05.720
very, very, very hard on that, and I think
were largely successful, as David had mentioned.

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:11.039
You know, the first thing we did was to agree
to the referral of the genocide in Sudan to

00:29:11.039 --> 00:29:17.000
the ICC. I and the war crimes ambassadors
gave numerous speeches emphasizing that we

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:23.000
shared the goal of the International Criminal
Court. We offered to provide intelligence

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:28.650
to the prosecutor. We waived restrictions
on assistance to countries that had become

00:29:28.650 --> 00:29:33.519
party to the ICC. And then, by the end of
the administration, we then took the very

00:29:33.519 --> 00:29:38.750
unusual step to become, frankly, the biggest
defender of the court, when other countries

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:43.710
who had been supporters of it -- namely, France,
and then China, who had not been a supporter

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:52.610
-- wanted to defer the prosecution of Bashir
of Sudan under so-called Article 16 of the

00:29:52.610 --> 00:29:57.610
Rome Statute that allows a deferral of a prosecution.

00:29:57.610 --> 00:30:01.500
Secretary Rice (in/and ?) our administration
did not want to let Bashir off the hook, and

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:08.899
she made it clear that we would veto, in the
Security Council, any effort to defer that

00:30:08.899 --> 00:30:14.309
prosecution. So, suddenly the Bush administration,
which had started in 2001 as appearing to

00:30:14.309 --> 00:30:19.769
be anti-ICC, had actually become in many ways
its supporter.

00:30:19.769 --> 00:30:25.840
So I guess I would say the overall message
of the second term was a more constructive,

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:31.860
pragmatic approach of peaceful coexistence
in which we emphasize that we still have deep

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:37.720
concerns about the court's application to
United States, but that we could work together

00:30:37.720 --> 00:30:39.820
with it. So that was sort of where we left
the --

00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:45.570
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Two seconds. I strongly support
what John just said about that final phase

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:49.899
in particular. I actually got drawn in by
Ambassador Williamson at one point about this

00:30:49.899 --> 00:30:56.600
whole issue of the Security Council being
pressured by the African Union to suspend

00:30:56.600 --> 00:31:03.840
the Bashir indictment for a year at least.
And I can tell you that the Bush administration,

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:09.230
because it held so firm on that issue of not
suspending it, actually ultimately pulled

00:31:09.230 --> 00:31:14.140
France and Britain along with it, and that
was the three-member, you know, group they

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:17.200
needed to really block it within the Security
Council.

00:31:17.200 --> 00:31:23.330
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: At this time I'd like to invite
members to join the conversation with their

00:31:23.330 --> 00:31:29.350
questions. There are folks with microphones.
Please -- why don't we start here -- wait

00:31:29.350 --> 00:31:35.600
for the microphone, speak directly into it,
stand, state your name and affiliation. Please

00:31:35.600 --> 00:31:40.580
limit yourself to one question and keep it
concise to allow as many members as possible

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:41.809
to speak.

00:31:41.809 --> 00:31:42.820
Got all that? OK.

00:31:42.820 --> 00:31:47.690
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: I think so. Hi. My name is -- (inaudible).
I have just gotten back from Afghanistan with

00:31:47.690 --> 00:31:53.159
the U.S. Institute of Peace. And my question
is, broadly -- this is a little bit outside

00:31:53.159 --> 00:32:00.070
of your particular jurisdiction, but how do
you reconcile America's purported support

00:32:00.070 --> 00:32:04.840
for accountability for war crimes and other
violations of international law with what

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:09.669
we've done in Iraq and Afghanistan and the
approach that has been -- you know, we cannot

00:32:09.669 --> 00:32:11.169
have peace and justice?

00:32:11.169 --> 00:32:14.899
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: That's the better version of the
question I was trying to ask. (Laughter.)

00:32:14.899 --> 00:32:18.450
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Well, let me take that on, if
I may.

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:26.720
I will be the first to admit that when you
look very objectively at both Iraq and Afghanistan,

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:32.129
there are natural questions that arise about
-- particularly in the field of war crimes

00:32:32.129 --> 00:32:39.450
-- as to the conduct not only of the American
military but the militaries of other countries,

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:45.690
where this issue has cropped up. There have
been a lot of court martials and a lot of

00:32:45.690 --> 00:32:51.940
investigations of the conduct of American
soldiers in both theaters.

00:32:51.940 --> 00:32:56.809
First -- and this is very short, but the first
point is this. I think if you empirically

00:32:56.809 --> 00:33:03.639
look at the corpus of what the U.S. military
justice system has done to review American

00:33:03.639 --> 00:33:12.460
conduct in Iraq and Afghanistan, I personally
find some of it lacking. I find some deep

00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:19.481
difficulties in how we have judged ourselves
in the field. And that's a long story, but

00:33:19.481 --> 00:33:25.470
I just think there are some difficulties there
and they were amplified last week with the

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:34.629
Haditha sentence that came down, the three-month
suspended sentence on the Haditha massacre

00:33:34.629 --> 00:33:40.330
in Afghanistan. And I think all of that takes
much more scrutiny.

00:33:40.330 --> 00:33:47.840
I will also point out that the ICC, the International
Criminal Court, has under preliminary examination

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:53.409
Afghanistan as a matter, a situation that
has been raised with the court. They've had

00:33:53.409 --> 00:33:59.779
it under consideration for many years now.
There's been no prospect that it will rise

00:33:59.779 --> 00:34:05.020
to the level of a formal investigation yet.
But I think it is important to recognize that

00:34:05.020 --> 00:34:13.780
Afghanistan is a state party to the Rome Statute
and therefore it is ripe for investigation

00:34:13.780 --> 00:34:15.740
if so merited.

00:34:15.740 --> 00:34:22.950
And I have wondered myself whether the first
non-African situation that is taken up by

00:34:22.950 --> 00:34:28.290
the international court may indeed one day
be Afghanistan. And that's not just the United

00:34:28.290 --> 00:34:33.640
States, believe me. I think the primary group
there is the Taliban and what the Taliban

00:34:33.640 --> 00:34:37.800
has been doing. But it obviously does involve
the United States military.

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:39.840
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: John, do you want to address this
issue?

00:34:39.840 --> 00:34:43.060
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: Well, briefly, just so we can
get to other questions. I mean, I agree with

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:49.110
your point, which is that there is a view
around the rest of the world that the United

00:34:49.110 --> 00:34:55.460
States feels that this is sort of for others
but not for us, and that makes our job promoting

00:34:55.460 --> 00:35:02.380
international justice at the international
tribunal level more difficult. On the other

00:35:02.380 --> 00:35:07.220
hand, these tribunals, like the International
Criminal Court, are supposed to be for the

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:15.690
most egregious and systematic crimes, you
know, because one American soldier, even if

00:35:15.690 --> 00:35:19.640
he's done something which clearly is a war
crime, that really doesn't make that person

00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:23.760
a good candidate for the International Criminal
Court.

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:28.060
It makes our nervous -- our military, on the
other hand, extremely nervous because frankly,

00:35:28.060 --> 00:35:33.150
all of these individual incidents do get referred
to the International Criminal Court. Largely,

00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:37.920
the prosecutor says, you know, that's really
not what we're here for; we're here for genocide,

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:43.690
mass, systematic war crimes. But then if there
then (ends up ?) somebody who essentially

00:35:43.690 --> 00:35:48.780
looks like they're either just not getting
prosecuted at all or get six months or just

00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:53.010
a couple of years for something that looks
truly horrible in Iraq or Afghanistan, like

00:35:53.010 --> 00:36:00.290
Haditha, it then looks like we have a double
standard. But it takes some explaining for

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:01.720
us to -- (inaudible).

00:36:01.720 --> 00:36:09.010
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Another question? Yes, sir, right
-- do you -- oh, yeah.

00:36:09.010 --> 00:36:14.260
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Conrad Harper. I'm a retired
member of Simpson Thatcher &amp; Bartlett. I want

00:36:14.260 --> 00:36:15.770
to the thank both David and --

00:36:15.770 --> 00:36:17.210
&gt;&gt;MR. : And a former legal adviser.

00:36:17.210 --> 00:36:23.011
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thank you -- for an excellent
series of statements. I want to ask you a

00:36:23.011 --> 00:36:26.010
question that's terribly unfair, but it has
--

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:27.030
&gt;&gt;MR. : (Off mic) -- David?

00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:30.840
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: (Laughs.) It has to do with
the title of this presentation; you know,

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:38.140
the evolution of international justice in
the context of the synthesis of law and politics.

00:36:38.140 --> 00:36:45.690
The issue of our position on torture during
the last decade, it seemed to me, put us at

00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:51.200
war with much of what we had said not only
internationally but by way of treaty and obligations

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:59.110
thereunder. And I wonder to what degree you
think politics and law are necessarily at

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:03.330
war as well in this area.

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:07.780
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: That is a huge question. I suppose
that's probably more directed at me, Conrad,

00:37:07.780 --> 00:37:08.780
although David can --

00:37:08.780 --> 00:37:11.000
Q (?): I think it's safe to say it's directed
at you, yes. (Laughter.)

00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:12.350
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: -- David can wade in.

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:23.340
It's a huge question. You know, I think certainly
the debate about torture has hurt the United

00:37:23.340 --> 00:37:32.720
States and has hurt our reputation and image
around the world. You know, I just don't see,

00:37:32.720 --> 00:37:38.420
wherever you are on the spectrum about the
CIA program and interrogations and all of

00:37:38.420 --> 00:37:44.160
those things, that you can't concede that.
Whether it was -- what the United States said

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:52.740
was justified, people can argue back and forth,
and you can argue whether the CIA program

00:37:52.740 --> 00:38:00.590
was consistent with U.S. criminal law, which
required an intent requirement to cause severe

00:38:00.590 --> 00:38:05.240
physical pain or suffering and whether it
was consistent with the Convention Against

00:38:05.240 --> 00:38:10.810
Torture. And that's a debate that continues
to rage to this day.

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:17.200
But it certainly, in my view, at least, and
certainly when I was legal adviser and was

00:38:17.200 --> 00:38:23.620
trying, in the second term of the Bush administration,
to promote a conception of international law,

00:38:23.620 --> 00:38:28.870
of commitment to rule of law and international
justice, you know, the fact that, you know,

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:36.790
the United States was itself accused of torture
-- and I had to be the head of the U.S. delegation

00:38:36.790 --> 00:38:43.580
presenting the U.S. report to the convention
-- to the Committee on Torture in 2006. That

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:47.650
was not one of my most enjoyable moments.

00:38:47.650 --> 00:38:54.260
But, you know, these are things that we had
to do, was both to try to promote international

00:38:54.260 --> 00:39:01.800
justice around the world while -- explain
the decisions that the U.S. government had

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:02.860
taken.

00:39:02.860 --> 00:39:09.950
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: And I can just jump in, if I may,
from the 1990s and say that I think, Conrad,

00:39:09.950 --> 00:39:17.710
one of the sort of politics-versus-justice
dilemmas we had back then was actually related,

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:23.730
of course, to the International Criminal Court
issue, where not only the Defense Department

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:28.540
but, quite frankly, the State Department and
the National Security Council and certainly

00:39:28.540 --> 00:39:37.710
Capitol Hill, I think they were deeply concerned
at times that the freedom we have to develop

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:45.680
our foreign policy and to execute our foreign
policy would be unduly influenced by the introduction

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:52.380
of possible liability before the International
Criminal court. In other words, that that

00:39:52.380 --> 00:39:58.690
legal liability would impinge upon effective
foreign policy-making.

00:39:58.690 --> 00:40:05.280
And there was an argument that they wanted
to separate that and not expose our policymakers

00:40:05.280 --> 00:40:12.010
on military policy as well as on foreign policy
to the influence of potential liability under

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:15.390
the International Criminal Court. I mean,
that's -- that was the argument at the time.

00:40:15.390 --> 00:40:18.900
And I had to grapple with that quite a bit.

00:40:18.900 --> 00:40:21.200
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Over there.

00:40:21.200 --> 00:40:27.810
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Jeff Laurenti with the Century
Foundation. During last fall's heated debate

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:35.230
at the U.N. about potential recognition of
the statehood of Palestine, the big bogeyman

00:40:35.230 --> 00:40:40.180
that agitated the Israelis was the fear that
they would sign -- the Palestinians would

00:40:40.180 --> 00:40:46.380
then sign, by virtue of statehood, the ICC
Rome Statute, and that then Israel would be

00:40:46.380 --> 00:40:54.390
opened up to liability for all kinds of potential
charges. Was that always an exaggerated fantasy?

00:40:54.390 --> 00:41:01.380
Or to what extent was that in fact a real
concern, a real risk of facing international

00:41:01.380 --> 00:41:03.230
legal action with Palestinian ratification?

00:41:03.230 --> 00:41:05.930
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: David, do you want to take that?

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:09.910
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Yeah. Thanks, Jeff, very much.
Well, let me just take you back for a moment.

00:41:09.910 --> 00:41:16.830
In the 1990s, I worked extremely closely with
Alan Baker, on just a(n) almost weekly -- daily

00:41:16.830 --> 00:41:24.060
basis, it seemed at times, in terms of our
approach to the Rome Statute, to the negotiations,

00:41:24.060 --> 00:41:29.090
and then ultimately to the signing. You know,
the United States was the 138th nation to

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:34.730
sign the Rome Statute on December 31st, 2000;
Israel was the 139th. The Israeli ambassador

00:41:34.730 --> 00:41:39.090
was standing right behind me because we were
sort of coordinating our moves towards that

00:41:39.090 --> 00:41:45.210
table. And once he saw me sign it, then he
had authority from the state of Israel to

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:47.820
sign the treaty.

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:53.610
So we had a very -- and the back story in
Israel to the signing of the Rome Statute,

00:41:53.610 --> 00:42:00.380
the little that I know of it, I think is also
fascinating: how Holocaust groups brought

00:42:00.380 --> 00:42:05.680
considerable influence and pressure on the
government to actually step up to the plate

00:42:05.680 --> 00:42:10.620
and recognize the importance of the International
Criminal Court as a follow-on, frankly, to

00:42:10.620 --> 00:42:14.710
Nuremberg and the legacy of the Holocaust.

00:42:14.710 --> 00:42:22.790
But I think in terms of the Palestinian statehood
issue, Jeff, I for years have been talking

00:42:22.790 --> 00:42:30.380
to my friends in Israel and elsewhere that,
if there's one thing that the peace process

00:42:30.380 --> 00:42:36.080
should incorporate -- and, frankly, one thing
that should stimulate some acceleration in

00:42:36.080 --> 00:42:42.950
the peace process, given this reality -- it
is an understanding that is codified into

00:42:42.950 --> 00:42:48.470
the arrangement, the peace agreement between
Israel and the Palestinian authorities that

00:42:48.470 --> 00:42:54.859
sort of stabilizes the liability net of the
ICC, because you know that the Palestinians

00:42:54.859 --> 00:43:00.610
-- I'm almost certain they will rush towards
signature and ratification of the Rome Statute

00:43:00.610 --> 00:43:04.820
the first chance they get. And there are legal
scholars right now that say right now they

00:43:04.820 --> 00:43:10.890
have the right to do it right now. I mean,
so we have to be cognizant of this.

00:43:10.890 --> 00:43:16.420
But I think the arrangement between Israel
and Palestinian should be: Look, the peace

00:43:16.420 --> 00:43:24.561
-- the peace agreement will have an agreement
between our two nations that the Palestinian

00:43:24.561 --> 00:43:30.600
authorities will not bring to the ICC any
alleged atrocity crimes under the Rome Statute

00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:35.600
that have occurred prior to the date of the
peace agreement. So we have a bilateral agreement

00:43:35.600 --> 00:43:42.850
between our nations that the past will not
be brought before the ICC. In exchange for

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:47.570
that, we, Israel, you know, are going to move
this peace process forward, with all the other

00:43:47.570 --> 00:43:51.090
conditions that have to be met, of course.

00:43:51.090 --> 00:43:56.700
And -- but that does mean that Israel has
to accept the reality, Jeff, that in the future

00:43:56.700 --> 00:44:04.570
there will be a Palestinian state that will
doubtless join the Rome Statute. And Israel

00:44:04.570 --> 00:44:08.410
has to factor that into its future foreign
policy.

00:44:08.410 --> 00:44:14.780
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Yes, sir. Will you wait for the
microphone?

00:44:14.780 --> 00:44:15.980
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Bill Luers. Congratulations.

00:44:15.980 --> 00:44:19.470
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Hi, Bill. This guy used to be
my boss. (Laughter.)

00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:20.490
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Yeah. (Chuckles.)

00:44:20.490 --> 00:44:27.780
I want to confess, John, that I -- my first
effort to smoke pot, I couldn't -- (laughter)

00:44:27.780 --> 00:44:33.400
-- I couldn't inhale, and I never got high
-- (laughter) -- and I have a witness here.

00:44:33.400 --> 00:44:34.660
(Laughter.)

00:44:34.660 --> 00:44:46.310
David, talk a bit about the contrast -- or
the conflict between trying to get a dictator/genocide-committer

00:44:46.310 --> 00:44:55.500
out of office quickly, and the court's inhibition
to make that happen. There was -- there have

00:44:55.500 --> 00:45:00.610
been cases in the past where we've been able
to get a Marcos out of the country without

00:45:00.610 --> 00:45:06.530
-- and the impunity issue didn't arise, because
it was better to get him out. And there was

00:45:06.530 --> 00:45:12.370
an effort, which I was involved with, to get
Gadhafi out quickly in -- right after things

00:45:12.370 --> 00:45:17.970
began to come apart. There's -- I still believe
there was an opportunity to do that. But once

00:45:17.970 --> 00:45:23.751
the court did its deed, it was -- became much
more difficult. Talk a bit about that. You

00:45:23.751 --> 00:45:24.751
and I have talked about this in the past.

00:45:24.751 --> 00:45:25.751
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Sure.

00:45:25.751 --> 00:45:26.850
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: That's an interesting dilemma, yeah.

00:45:26.850 --> 00:45:32.840
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: No, absolutely, and it's a classic
dilemma here of: Do you achieve peace and

00:45:32.840 --> 00:45:42.870
stability by essentially enabling someone
who is responsible for atrocity crimes to

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:48.550
live in sanctuary someplace outside of the
scope of the law?

00:45:48.550 --> 00:45:54.710
I think the last 17 years have been very transformational
on that issue. When I came into office in

00:45:54.710 --> 00:46:01.030
my position in 1993 with the Clinton administration,
one of our first tasks was not only this issue

00:46:01.030 --> 00:46:06.400
of the Yugoslav Tribunal, but we were actually
having crisis meetings on Haiti and what to

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:15.180
do about the situation in Haiti. And the proposal
on the table to ensure that General Cedras

00:46:15.180 --> 00:46:21.310
could be flown ultimately to Panama and live
happily ever after on the shores of Panama

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:26.770
was a pretty noncontroversial proposal. I
mean, there weren't people questioning: Well,

00:46:26.770 --> 00:46:32.000
should he be brought to justice? It was a
-- it was a live option on the table. And

00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:38.410
ultimately, in 1994, guess what? General Cedras
was flown to Panama, and lives happily ever

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:45.900
after, despite calls in Panama for his accountability
-- and I think they're legitimate calls; it's

00:46:45.900 --> 00:46:49.010
just that he's now in a sanctuary.

00:46:49.010 --> 00:46:55.920
My view is this. We have actually passed a
transformational moment where, by the commission

00:46:55.920 --> 00:47:03.020
of atrocity crimes, the leadership that is
responsible for it simply does not get a free

00:47:03.020 --> 00:47:11.170
pass. Now, that's a -- that's a generational
transformational moment in history, where

00:47:11.170 --> 00:47:17.290
you cannot any longer presume that if someone
is responsible for the deaths of tens of thousands

00:47:17.290 --> 00:47:21.920
-- hundreds of thousands of people, you're
going to arrange it so that that individual

00:47:21.920 --> 00:47:24.440
never faces justice for it.

00:47:24.440 --> 00:47:32.390
Now, I say that perfectly realizing that we're
going to have situations where deals are going

00:47:32.390 --> 00:47:38.680
to be cut. And I think even with Gadhafi,
I was, you know, publicly, on CNN -- where

00:47:38.680 --> 00:47:46.210
I could only speak, because they were asking
for it -- but, you know, I was saying on CNN:

00:47:46.210 --> 00:47:51.590
Look, you -- Gadhafi has not been indicted
yet; he has every opportunity now to cut a

00:47:51.590 --> 00:47:58.920
deal and to find a sanctuary; and quite frankly,
great, do it; get out of there; find your

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:03.860
sanctuary; and the law may not be able to
reach you in that sanctuary. It doesn't mean

00:48:03.860 --> 00:48:09.721
the law's wrong; it doesn't mean that Ocampo
will not indict you. He may indict you. But

00:48:09.721 --> 00:48:12.960
you'll be in your sanctuary, even if he does.

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:16.760
He did not seize that opportunity. He had
a clear opportunity.

00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:21.570
So my view is, look, you know, we're not -- we're
not dumb about this. We're not going to wait

00:48:21.570 --> 00:48:26.661
forever. If you wait until you're actually
indicted and then you say, oh, please, give

00:48:26.661 --> 00:48:32.570
me my sanctuary now, guess what? I think we've
reached a stage where it's too late to give

00:48:32.570 --> 00:48:33.570
you that sanctuary.

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:38.310
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: This is obviously an intense moral
and political dilemma of, you know: Do you

00:48:38.310 --> 00:48:43.880
give someone a pass to get them out of office?
Who decides that? Who's in charge?

00:48:43.880 --> 00:48:53.121
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Well, John, I want you to answer
this, but -- (laughter) -- I would -- I would

00:48:53.121 --> 00:48:59.730
just say that I have logically somehow drawn
this line in my mind where: You're not indicted

00:48:59.730 --> 00:49:06.490
yet, so talk to us. What's the deal? Where
are you going? But the moment that you actually

00:49:06.490 --> 00:49:11.100
get indicted, that changes the whole -- the
whole picture.

00:49:11.100 --> 00:49:16.340
And it doesn't mean that you're avoiding international
justice; international justice will still

00:49:16.340 --> 00:49:23.590
try to pursue you. But I'm not saying that
for purposes of actually achieving peace and

00:49:23.590 --> 00:49:29.470
stability in that country -- if the individual
is not yet indicted, it's perfectly fair to

00:49:29.470 --> 00:49:37.030
be talking to him and trying to determine
how do we resolve this situation for the betterment

00:49:37.030 --> 00:49:38.910
of the populace which is under siege.

00:49:38.910 --> 00:49:42.370
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: This gets actually into a very
interesting point about the independence of

00:49:42.370 --> 00:49:48.530
the ICC prosecutor. And I think, as people
may know, one of the real concerns about -- that

00:49:48.530 --> 00:49:53.970
the United States had was that the ICC prosecutor
is completely independent and really beholden

00:49:53.970 --> 00:49:55.240
to no one.

00:49:55.240 --> 00:50:00.320
One of the concerns in, frankly, both administrations
had been -- David would have been there with

00:50:00.320 --> 00:50:05.420
respect to President Clinton -- was, gee,
if you liked our independent prosecutors in

00:50:05.420 --> 00:50:10.291
the United States -- who, of course, had gone
after President Clinton -- you know, look

00:50:10.291 --> 00:50:13.230
what worldwide independent prosecutors can
be like.

00:50:13.230 --> 00:50:14.620
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Well, I made that argument, you
know. (Chuckles.)

00:50:14.620 --> 00:50:22.030
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: And the one problem in this is
that the ICC prosecutor may not, in preparing

00:50:22.030 --> 00:50:26.810
an indictment, necessarily be talking to the
rest of the countries in the world or to the

00:50:26.810 --> 00:50:31.610
Security Council. If the person's smart, they
really ought to be doing that.

00:50:31.610 --> 00:50:35.630
But I remember when I actually raised this
in Europe at one point, is -- you know, you're

00:50:35.630 --> 00:50:40.980
not going to want the prosecutor to be indicting
a world leader while all the rest of the countries

00:50:40.980 --> 00:50:45.790
or the secretary-general or a small group
of them are working to try to get that person

00:50:45.790 --> 00:50:51.280
out, and out pops a(n) indictment that undermines
everything.

00:50:51.280 --> 00:50:56.640
And at least the way the Rome Statute is set
up, and the way many of the countries in the

00:50:56.640 --> 00:51:03.550
world want it to work is, they don't want
the ICC prosecutor to be engaged in a political

00:51:03.550 --> 00:51:09.620
balancing. This is all such a pure view of
has a particular individual committed a war

00:51:09.620 --> 00:51:15.240
crime that I'm not going to talk to the secretary-general
and say, is it OK if I indict this person

00:51:15.240 --> 00:51:18.660
tomorrow, or are you about to actually move
him somewhere else?

00:51:18.660 --> 00:51:21.730
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: And I would just jump in very
quickly to say, on Libya, of course, there

00:51:21.730 --> 00:51:25.330
was a distinction; it was the Security Council
referral of this whole matter --

00:51:25.330 --> 00:51:26.330
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: Right.

00:51:26.330 --> 00:51:29.790
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: -- to the ICC. So the prosecutor
is sitting there with the Security Council

00:51:29.790 --> 00:51:36.640
saying, please investigate and, if merited,
indict. So it's a slightly different dynamic.

00:51:36.640 --> 00:51:39.700
The prosecutor is moving forward with a Security
Council mandate.

00:51:39.700 --> 00:51:42.610
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Can we talk about Libya for a minute,
or will you take another question?

00:51:42.610 --> 00:51:46.970
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: You know, talk about Libya quickly
-- quickly about Libya. Well, David writes

00:51:46.970 --> 00:51:50.340
600-page books; I write 750-word op-eds.

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:56.420
And I wrote an op-ed -- Gadhafi, of course,
is no longer going to go before the ICC, but

00:51:56.420 --> 00:52:03.380
there are two other people who were indicted
by the ICC prosecutor: Seif al-Gadhafi, Gadhafi's

00:52:03.380 --> 00:52:09.460
son, and his intelligence chief, Senoussi,
who have been captured and are now alive in

00:52:09.460 --> 00:52:16.400
Libya. And there is essentially a tug of war
going on right now between the -- at least

00:52:16.400 --> 00:52:20.930
the -- some in the international community
and the Libyans as to where they ought to

00:52:20.930 --> 00:52:22.170
be tried.

00:52:22.170 --> 00:52:28.160
I think Prosecutor Ocampo has said he's fine
with having them prosecuted in Libya. But

00:52:28.160 --> 00:52:34.960
there are others who say that there is not
a sufficiently well-developed judicial system

00:52:34.960 --> 00:52:41.970
in Libya, it would be unfair to allow them
to be tried there, it wouldn't be real justice,

00:52:41.970 --> 00:52:46.640
and they therefore must be tried before the
International Criminal Court, and Libya has

00:52:46.640 --> 00:52:48.340
to turn them over to the International Criminal
Court.

00:52:48.340 --> 00:52:49.340
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: What's your view?

00:52:49.340 --> 00:52:54.740
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: Well, what I said in the op-ed
is that if the Libyans want to try them, the

00:52:54.740 --> 00:52:59.160
-- we need to defer to their desire to do
that. The International Criminal Court is

00:52:59.160 --> 00:53:04.640
a complementary court to complement a national
system. If we think there's a problem about

00:53:04.640 --> 00:53:10.340
fairness, which I think there certainly is,
the answer is not to paternalistically say,

00:53:10.340 --> 00:53:15.530
well, we'll take these people away from you;
we'll handle it. It's, on my view, to help

00:53:15.530 --> 00:53:21.580
the Libyans to do it right, not only to ensure
that they have fair trials in the first instance,

00:53:21.580 --> 00:53:26.850
but ultimately to develop the rule of law
in Libya over the longer term, to develop

00:53:26.850 --> 00:53:27.870
those institutions.

00:53:27.870 --> 00:53:29.100
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: What do you think about those -- (inaudible)?

00:53:29.100 --> 00:53:35.770
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: I would -- I'd just respectfully
disagree. Libya has been referred by the Security

00:53:35.770 --> 00:53:44.380
Council to the ICC. And it may be that the
prosecutor indeed is negotiating this issue

00:53:44.380 --> 00:53:49.120
with Libyan authorities. I don't know the
details of those negotiations, but I think

00:53:49.120 --> 00:53:54.930
he's probably trying to draw some very clear
lines of -- you know, demonstrate to me that

00:53:54.930 --> 00:54:02.190
you in fact can have a credible trial with
due process, security, everything else that

00:54:02.190 --> 00:54:06.771
-- I mean, for some reason, the Security Council
referred Libya to the ICC, so there is a premise

00:54:06.771 --> 00:54:07.771
there --

00:54:07.771 --> 00:54:08.860
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: Why should they have to demonstrate
that? I mean, it's their country, David.

00:54:08.860 --> 00:54:09.860
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Because --

00:54:09.860 --> 00:54:13.590
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: Why shouldn't they be able to
prosecute their nationals in their country?

00:54:13.590 --> 00:54:18.440
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Because the court is seized with
this situation, and it's seized under its

00:54:18.440 --> 00:54:25.020
statute by the Security Council. So I'm looking
at this from the equity of the United Nations,

00:54:25.020 --> 00:54:31.260
the Security Council, the equity of the International
Criminal Court, and not simply the preferences

00:54:31.260 --> 00:54:35.730
of the Libyan government. This is already
within the jurisdiction of that court, so

00:54:35.730 --> 00:54:37.400
I'm respecting that jurisdiction.

00:54:37.400 --> 00:54:41.060
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: But the Rome Statute is a complementary
system intended to complement --

00:54:41.060 --> 00:54:42.060
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Right. And that --

00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:43.060
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: -- the national justice system.

00:54:43.060 --> 00:54:49.850
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: And that is a process. It is not
a conclusion. It is a process that has to

00:54:49.850 --> 00:54:55.660
reach a determination as to whether or not
the Libyan courts are, in fact, capable and

00:54:55.660 --> 00:55:03.680
willing to prosecute these two individuals
in a manner that holds up under the requirements

00:55:03.680 --> 00:55:06.151
of the Rome Statute, quite frankly. So --

00:55:06.151 --> 00:55:07.460
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: But shouldn't the answer then
be -- (inaudible) -- right?

00:55:07.460 --> 00:55:12.180
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: There is no automatic answer that
the Libyans automatically get them. It's a

00:55:12.180 --> 00:55:18.610
process that has to be determined. And quite
frankly, I am of the view that I'd be interested

00:55:18.610 --> 00:55:23.500
in the views of the Security Council as to
whether or not they are completely delegating

00:55:23.500 --> 00:55:28.650
to Libya the prosecution of individuals that
a year ago they were extremely interested

00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:32.060
in the International Criminal Court investigating
and prosecuting.

00:55:32.060 --> 00:55:37.580
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Why don't we have one more question
from the audience? And then I have a question.

00:55:37.580 --> 00:55:40.670
Yes, sir. Right here.

00:55:40.670 --> 00:55:43.670
&gt;&gt;BELLINGER: You told me we were not supposed
to agree with you -- (inaudible).

00:55:43.670 --> 00:55:47.500
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: I've been -- thank you for following
my directions. Please, stand up.

00:55:47.500 --> 00:55:51.970
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Jim Raymond, International Institute
for Legal Writing and Reasoning. I wondered

00:55:51.970 --> 00:55:57.060
if the situation in Libya doesn't have a precedent
in Rwanda, where the Rwandan courts wanted

00:55:57.060 --> 00:56:02.780
to try these genocidaires, and the United
Nations said, no, no, no, wait; you got to

00:56:02.780 --> 00:56:07.490
develop your courts first and prove to us
that you're capable of doing it according

00:56:07.490 --> 00:56:12.311
to international standards. And so how do
you feel about the way that was handled? Was

00:56:12.311 --> 00:56:16.360
it a good precedent for what we are doing
and should do in Libya?

00:56:16.360 --> 00:56:21.000
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Well, that issue's -- really came
to a fore within the last four years or so

00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:27.310
when the Rwandan government really did try
to make a case for taking a number of these

00:56:27.310 --> 00:56:35.600
cases off the hands of the tribunal. And initially,
they failed in convincing the judges of the

00:56:35.600 --> 00:56:42.520
Rwanda tribunal to transfer the cases to Rwandan
courts because of certain issues, which really,

00:56:42.520 --> 00:56:48.980
at the end of the day, proved to be very narrow
issues in Rwanda, but still of some importance

00:56:48.980 --> 00:56:54.660
to the tribunal in terms of the existence
of those situations.

00:56:54.660 --> 00:57:01.940
Just last December, just a couple of -- frankly,
a month ago, there is -- the appeals chamber

00:57:01.940 --> 00:57:09.450
of the Rwanda tribunal has approved the transfer
of a case to Rwandan courts. So we do now

00:57:09.450 --> 00:57:16.090
finally have it happening. There has been
a standard met that has satisfied the appeals

00:57:16.090 --> 00:57:20.440
judges of the Rwanda tribunal, as well as
the trial court judges, actually. I think

00:57:20.440 --> 00:57:28.260
the appeals judge was -- they were upholding
the trial chamber determination on this. So

00:57:28.260 --> 00:57:30.010
it finally did happen.

00:57:30.010 --> 00:57:34.070
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Council events end promptly, so
I -- we're not quite done. I -- just one question

00:57:34.070 --> 00:57:38.020
that occurred to me several times in reading
your book. And I apologize; it's not as high-minded

00:57:38.020 --> 00:57:43.750
as many of these issues. I mean, you were
dealing with the most unspeakable tragedies.

00:57:43.750 --> 00:57:49.270
You were going to places, seeing dead bodies
everywhere. Like -- how did you deal with

00:57:49.270 --> 00:57:51.180
that? What was it like?

00:57:51.180 --> 00:57:57.150
&gt;&gt;SCHEFFER: Well, as I write in the book,
it certainly creates a certain frame of mind

00:57:57.150 --> 00:58:04.060
about how you view the importance, I think,
of this work. And that is -- that is heightened,

00:58:04.060 --> 00:58:13.700
which is why I'm still engaged in it. And
I was -- I was actually, you know, willing,

00:58:13.700 --> 00:58:18.220
recognizing the difficulties, to jump back
into the Cambodian situation, because I think

00:58:18.220 --> 00:58:24.010
it's extremely important that the deaths of
1.7 million Cambodians in the 1970s be answered

00:58:24.010 --> 00:58:28.830
for in some credible process.

00:58:28.830 --> 00:58:34.290
But in dealing with it emotionally, I will
tell you that after your second or third or

00:58:34.290 --> 00:58:41.230
fourth mass grave or atrocity site, you can
actually start to get somewhat used to it;

00:58:41.230 --> 00:58:47.790
I mean, understand how many humanitarian workers,
medical workers and even peacekeepers actually

00:58:47.790 --> 00:58:54.450
start to get used to that. Of course, soldiers
do. But it is something you can -- you can

00:58:54.450 --> 00:58:59.690
-- you can -- you can walk into and deal with.

00:58:59.690 --> 00:59:05.010
But I could never get used to seeing children
in this -- in this situation. It simply wasn't

00:59:05.010 --> 00:59:12.210
tolerable. And it wrecked -- it wrecked me
every time I saw it.

00:59:12.210 --> 00:59:30.040
&gt;&gt;TOOBIN: Thank you all for coming.

